<title>DENMARK: Danish Nov North Sea oil production rises 0.7 pct.</title>
<headline>Danish Nov North Sea oil production rises 0.7 pct.</headline>
<dateline>COPENHAGEN 1996-12-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>Danish Underground Consortium (DUC) North Sea oil production rose 0.7 percent in November from October, operator A.P. Moeller Group said on Thursday.</p>
<p>It said oil production was 937,000 tonnes in November or 229,238 barrels per day (bpd), compared with 227,620 bpd in October.</p>
<p>DUC is responsible for virtually all Denmark's North Sea oil and gas output. Denmark is the third-biggest producer in western Europe after Norway and Britain.</p>
<p>Production data (figures in 1,000 bpd):</p>
<p> Field			Nov 96	 Oct 96	  Nov 95</p>
<p> Dan			   68.0	    66.6	    65.1</p>
<p> Kraka			  4.4	     5.2	     7.1</p>
<p> Regnar			 0.0	     0.8	     1.0</p>
<p> Gorm			  50.2	    53.5	    39.1</p>
<p> Skjold			36.7	    32.1	    33.9</p>
<p> Rolf			   3.3	     3.9	     4.3</p>
<p> Dagmar			 0.3	     0.4	     0.5</p>
<p> Tyra			  23.8	    23.7	    29.6</p>
<p> Valdemar		     2.8	     2.2	     1.9</p>
<p> Roar			   8.2	     8.2		 -</p>
<p> Svend			 31.5	    30.4		 -</p>
<p> Total			229.2	   227.6	   182.5</p>
<p>A.P. Moeller Group owns 39 percent of DUC. Its two partners,</p>
<p>Royal Dutch/Shell Group and Texaco Inc</p>
<p>own 46 percent and 15 percent respectively.</p>
<p>-- Chris Follett, Copenhagen newsroom +45-33-969652</p>
</text>
